  🔥 LAYER 5 — PROBLEM PATTERNS BY FEEL
When you read a problem — it FEELS like one of these
  FEEL: "find if something exists"
→ SET membership check

FEEL: "find two numbers that sum to X"
→ DICT complement lookup
  seen[target - num] check

FEEL: "find best/longest/shortest subarray"
→ SLIDING WINDOW
  fixed if k given, variable if condition

FEEL: "sum between positions L and R"
→ PREFIX SUM
  prefix[R+1] - prefix[L]

FEEL: "next greater/smaller element"
→ MONOTONIC STACK
  decreasing stack → next greater
  increasing stack → next smaller

FEEL: "process tree level by level"
→ BFS with queue
  freeze len(queue) each level

FEEL: "find path in tree"
→ DFS recursion
  if not node → return base

FEEL: "find in sorted array"
→ BINARY SEARCH
  left <= right
  eliminate half each step

FEEL: "K largest or K most frequent"
→ MIN HEAP size K
  Counter.most_common(k) shortcut

FEEL: "cycle or middle of linked list"
→ SLOW/FAST POINTERS
  fast 2x speed of slow

FEEL: "merge or count overlapping intervals"
→ SORT BY START first
  check next.start <= prev.end

FEEL: "median of stream"
→ TWO HEAPS
  small = max heap lower half
  large = min heap upper half
  💊 LAYER 6 — COMMON MISTAKES — NEVER AGAIN
  ❌ return inside loop
✅ build inside → return outside

❌ list.pop(0) for queue
✅ deque.popleft() → O(1)

❌ s.join(list)
✅ "".join(list)

❌ storing values in monotonic stack
✅ store INDICES → gives position + value

❌ left < right in binary search
✅ left <= right → handles single element

❌ check only direct children in BST
✅ pass min/max boundaries down recursion

❌ BST: left > node
✅ BST: left < node < right

❌ pick tool before understanding
✅ understand → 3 questions → tool → pattern

❌ code before tracing
✅ paper trace → then code

❌ Google when stuck
✅ paper → think 20 min → then ask
